#git rollback commands are used to undo changes at different leves :
1) unstaged changes
1.1: undo changes in unstaged 
# git checkout --<file>
exp: git checkout --index.file (this will discard the changes permanently in the file)

2)staged changes
2.1 : undo changes in staged area
#git reset <file>
exp: git reset index.file (this file remains in directory but not in staging)

3)commited changes 
3.1: undo last commited changes
# git reset --soft HEAD~1 (HEAD~1 means one commit before the current HEAD.)
keeps the file staged, but uncommits the file 

3.2: undo the last commit & unstage the file 
   # git reset --mixed HEAD~1 (Keeps the channges in working dir but UNSTAGED.)
3.3 Undo the last commit and discard changes permanently
   # git reset --hard HEAD~1 (Removes the last commit & discard all changes.)
3.4 REVERT a commit (without removing history)
   # git revert <git id>
     exp: git revert a1b2c3d4

4) pushed commits
4.1: undo the pushed commits
# git revert <commit-hash>
4.2: force push(dangerous in production)
git reset --hard <commit-hash>
git push --force 
this rewrites history and can break other's clone.

